! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
! CTS bootstrap - runs on each SDA fabric device that participates in
! TrustSec (classification, propagation, or enforcement).
!
! Prerequisites (pushed by CC's standard provisioning, not here):
!   - radius server dnac-radius_<ise_ip>                 (from network_settings.aaa_servers)
!   - aaa group server radius dnac-client-radius-group   (CC creates from same config)
!   - aaa server radius dynamic-author / client <ise_ip> (CC creates for CoA)
!
! What this template adds:
!   1. A second 'aaa group server radius' (CTS-RADIUS-GROUP) that REUSES
!      the CC-managed 'radius server dnac-radius_<ise_ip>' entry but
!      applies its own 'ip vrf forwarding Mgmt-vrf' and
!      'ip radius source-interface GigabitEthernet0/0'. Required in this
!      lab because CC's standard 'dnac-client-radius-group' is sourced
!      from Loopback0 in the global VRF, which has no underlay route to
!      ISE in this CML topology. IOS allows multiple aaa-groups to wrap
!      the same radius server with different VRF/source-interface
!      overrides at the group level.
!
!      We do NOT create a second 'radius server' entry. IOS enforces
!      uniqueness on <address, auth-port, acct-port> across all radius
!      server entries, so a parallel server pointing at the same ISE IP
!      is rejected with '%Server already exists with same address port
!      combination'. Reusing the existing CC-managed server avoids this
!      and keeps the shared_secret in a single place (CC's config).
!
!      The netascode catc schema does not expose source-interface/vrf
!      knobs in network_settings.aaa_servers as of this version
!      (verified against catc/schemas/schema.yaml lines 362-381), so a
!      dayn_template is the SaC-supported escape hatch.
!
!   2. AAA method list 'CTS-AUTHZ-LIST' bound to CTS-RADIUS-GROUP
!      (replaces the previous 'group radius' binding, which inherited
!      the broken global 'ip radius source-interface Loopback0').
!
!   3. cts authorization list CTS-AUTHZ-LIST
!
!   4. dynamic-author CoA listener with Mgmt-vrf so ISE matrix updates
!      (RADIUS CoA, see ise/data/network_resources.nac.yaml
!      send_configuration_to_device_using: ENABLE_USING_COA) actually
!      reach the device. CC-pushed 'client 198.18.133.30 server-key'
!      without vrf is left alone - harmless, never matches inbound CoA.
!
!   5. Override of CC-managed 'aaa group server radius dnac-client-radius-group'
!      to use Mgmt-vrf and GigabitEthernet0/0 source. CTS PAC provisioning uses
!      EAP-FAST through the DEFAULT radius path (not CTS-RADIUS-GROUP). The
!      'cts authorization list' binding above only affects post-PAC authz
!      queries (env-data, SGACL download). Without fixing dnac-client-radius
!      -group itself, PAC provisioning packets get sourced from Loopback0 in
!      global VRF and never reach ISE - PAC provisioning hangs forever with
!      'aidlen = 0' in 'show cts provisioning'. Proven via per-device test on
!      EDGE01/EDGE02: this is the single change that unlocks PAC.
!
!   6. Remove 'automate-tester' probe from the radius server entry. CC pushes
!      'automate-tester username dummy ignore-acct-port probe-on' which sends
!      periodic dead-server-detection probes. Those probes are sourced from
!      the GLOBAL radius source-interface (Loopback0), not the per-group
!      override. In this lab the global VRF has no underlay route to ISE, so
!      the probes always fail. After 3 failures the server is marked DEAD
!      GLOBALLY - which also poisons CTS-RADIUS-GROUP because server state is
!      tracked per-server (address, port), not per-aaa-group. With the server
!      stuck DEAD, CTS-RADIUS-GROUP short-circuits and refuses to even send.
!      Removing the probe lets the server stay ALIVE and lets CTS-RADIUS-GROUP
!      actually use it for env-data and SGACL downloads via Mgmt-vrf.
!
! NOTE: cts credentials id/password is intentionally OMITTED here.
! That command is a privileged EXEC command (it writes to NVRAM keystore,
! not running-config), so it cannot be executed from configure-terminal
! mode where CC pushes templates. Pushing it via this template fails
! because the command is unknown in global-config mode.
!
! Push it manually on each fabric device after the first successful
! template deploy:
!   DEVICE# cts credentials id HOSTNAME password CTS_PASSWORD
! Stored in NVRAM keystore (not running-config), so this only needs to
! happen once per device lifetime (and persists across reboots).
!
! The 'device_name' and 'cts_password' variables declared in
! catc/data/templates.nac.yaml are kept (passed per-device in
! catc/data/multidomain_devices.nac.yaml) as placeholders / documentation
! of the required per-device values, even though this template body no
! longer consumes them. Future direction: if CC ever exposes an EXEC-
! command-push primitive, swap the manual step for a template line.
!
! The cts_password variable per-device in multidomain_devices.nac.yaml
! MUST equal the ISE NAD trust_sec.device_password for that device
! (currently C1sco12345 in ise/data/network_resources.nac.yaml) - that
! value is what the operator must type when manually running
! 'cts credentials id <hostname> password <cts_password>'.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------

aaa group server radius CTS-RADIUS-GROUP
 server name dnac-radius_198.18.133.30
 ip vrf forwarding Mgmt-vrf
 ip radius source-interface GigabitEthernet0/0
!
aaa authorization network CTS-AUTHZ-LIST group CTS-RADIUS-GROUP
cts authorization list CTS-AUTHZ-LIST
!
aaa server radius dynamic-author
 client 198.18.133.30 vrf Mgmt-vrf server-key C1sco12345
!
aaa group server radius dnac-client-radius-group
 no ip radius source-interface Loopback0
 ip vrf forwarding Mgmt-vrf
 ip radius source-interface GigabitEthernet0/0
!
radius server dnac-radius_198.18.133.30
 no automate-tester username dummy ignore-acct-port probe-on
